<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexandra Feodorovna (Alix of Hesse)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Alix of Hesse, Empress Alexandra of Russia, Empress Alexandra Feodorovna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexandra Feodorovna (1872–1918), born Princess Alix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, was the last Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>. A granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, she married Nicholas in 1894. Her reliance on the mystic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigori-rasputin">Grigori Rasputin</a>, who seemed to ease her son Alexei's haemophilia, damaged the monarchy during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. She was killed with her family by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> at Yekaterinburg in July 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexandra-feodorovna-alix-of-hesse.jpg" alt="Alexandra Feodorovna (Alix of Hesse)" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boasson and Eggler St. Petersburg Nevsky 24.Bain News Service, publisher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexandra_Fyodorovna_LOC_01137u.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alix was born on 6 June 1872 at the New Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, the sixth child and fourth daughter of Louis IV, Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-alice-of-the-united-kingdom">Princess Alice of the United Kingdom</a>, the second daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>. Her older brother Prince Friedrich, who had haemophilia, died after a fall in May 1873, when she was about a year old.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> wanted her to marry into the British royal house and in 1889 invited her to Balmoral to meet Prince Albert Victor, known as Eddy. He proposed, and in May 1890 Alix refused him, writing that she saw him only as a cousin. Her heart lay elsewhere. She had met the Russian heir, Nicholas, in 1884 at the wedding of her sister Elisabeth to Grand Duke Sergei Alexandrovich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, and her sister and brother-in-law favoured a match with him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Nicholas proposed in April 1894 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>. Alix at first declined, because marriage to the Russian heir required her to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> for Russian Orthodoxy, but she later accepted. After Alexander III died on 1 November 1894, she was received into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> the next day as Grand Duchess Alexandra Feodorovna. She and Nicholas married on 26 November 1894 in the Grand Church of the Winter Palace. They were crowned in the Dormition Cathedral in the Kremlin on 26 May 1896.</p><p>The couple had four daughters, Olga, Tatiana, Maria and Anastasia, and a son, Alexei, who had haemophilia. His illness shaped her relations with religious advisers and healers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigori-rasputin">Grigori Rasputin</a>, whom she came to believe could help the boy. She supported the preservation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a> and opposed some proposed reforms. Historians still debate how far she influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> and how far she was responsible for the decisions of his government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nicholas abdicated on 15 March 1917 (2 March in the old-style calendar). The family was held under house arrest at the Alexander Palace at Tsarskoye Selo and later moved to Yekaterinburg. On 17 July 1918 Alexandra, Nicholas and their five children were murdered by Bolshevik forces at the Ipatiev House, together with several members of their household.</p><p>The Russian Orthodox Church Outside Russia recognised the family as martyrs in 1981. Alexandra was reinterred with Nicholas and three of their children in the Peter and Paul Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1998, and the Russian Orthodox Church canonised her as a passion bearer in 2000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexandra Feodorovna (Alix of Hesse)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Louis IV married Princess Alice, daughter of Queen Victoria, and their daughters included <b>Alix</b>, who became Empress Alexandra of Russia in 1894, and Elisabeth, Grand Duchess Elizabeth Feodorovna.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Yekaterinburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.87,8.65&amp;year=1901" aria-label="Darmstadt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alexandra Feodorovna (Alix of Hesse)"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5475 5831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5475 5831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.87,8.65&amp;year=1901">What was here in 1901? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
